reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
Assume everyone plays the intended good line:
- the opener bids their own count (safe);
- turn 2 raises by a small step (about 98% safe);
- turn 3 is squeezed (loses about 50 to 92%);
- turn 4 takes whichever of call or raise is better.

Under that line the loser of each hand is mostly the turn-3 seat, and that seat is fixed by who lost the previous hand. Losses then rotate nearly cyclically: in 3 players, seats 3, 2, 1, 3, 2, 1 down to one die each. The final eliminations depend on where that cycle sits plus dice noise. The winner is therefore largely set by the random initial seating and the rolls, not by better inference.

Estimate of what decides the result:
- seat-driven position: about 50% of the result;
- dice variance at the decisive one-die end-game: about 25%;
- skill (reading bids, using one's own dice, bluffing on turn 4): about 25%.

There is genuine inference available on turn 4 and in calling stretched bids. But a strong player in the turn-3 seat still loses at least half the time, so good reasoning is not clearly favoured.

playable.evidence:
The rules are complete:
- valid opening range [max(1, floor(T/4)), T];
- raise range [q+1, T] with any face from 2 to 6;
- a forced Dudo when q = T;
- invalid actions mapped to defined outcomes;
- the counting rule (bid face plus ones);
- who loses the die;
- elimination and the next opener, including the case where the loser was eliminated;
- the re-roll and the recomputing of T.

The game is finite, with a hard cap of 40 rounds. The cap rule is fully arithmetic: most dice wins, ties draw, and an unresolved hand is ignored.

Each normal hand needs at least 2 rounds. Removing 7 of 8 dice (2 players at 4 dice each, or 4 players at 2 dice each) needs 7 hands. At about 3 or 4 rounds per hand that is roughly 21 to 28 rounds, so most games end naturally and the cap is a fallback.

The stated complexity is within limits:
- 16 of 20 steps;
- 1 of 4 choice steps;
- 13 of 20 attributes;
- 1 of 3 hidden elements;
- 1 random draw per round. The re-roll at the end of a hand happens in the same round as the Dudo call, and that round has no other draw.

There is 1 choice step per round, made by the single player to act. This sequential turn is intended and correct for Dudo, so no simultaneous poll is needed.

No board, judge, clock, trading or binding agreements are required. Hidden hands and numeric bids are both supported.

Talk: no separate talk phase is provided, and that is a sensible choice. The bids are themselves the public signalling channel, and every player wants a different outcome, so free text would only duplicate the bluffing the bids already carry.

The repeated-cooperation question does not apply, since the game has no cooperation dilemma.

Minor note: near round 40 a leader can try to keep a hand alive by raising, so that it is never resolved. This is limited by the +1 rule and the cap at T, and it is a legitimate planning consideration, not a gap in the rules.
